{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0139ynFGKfSKZWZLj2xSQ4DP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# skp\n2\t\n3\tSkp is a library of functions to help parsing text files.\n4\t\n5\tRather than answering the question \"which portion of the text match the pattern?\", skp\n6\tanswer to the question \"which portion of the text can be skipped to match the pattern?\".\n7\tThe difference is subtle but is there.\n8\t\n9\tSkp is *Unicode-aware* but, being the focus on parsing programming languages and data files,\n10\tthe uppercase/lower case conversion is only for the ASCII portion of the Latin alphabet.\n11\t\n12\tSome of the Unicode spaces are recognized like U+00A0 (NO-BREAK SPACE), U+2001 (EM QUAD), etc.\n13\t\n14\tDefault encoding is UTF-8, alternatively the ISO-8859-1 encoding can be used.\n15\t\n16\tSkp can be used at three increasing levels of complexity:\n17\t  - *Skipping*, where the focus is to identify portion of text that can be skipped according to a pattern;\n18\t  - *Scanning*, where a text is repeatedly checked against a set of patterns;\n19\t  - *Parsing*, where a Parsing Expression Grammar can be directly coded as a recursive descent parser.\n20\t\n21\t\n22\t## Patterns\n23\tTo match strings, `skp` uses *patterns*. \n24\tA *pattern* is a sequence of *recognizers* possibly separated by spaces.\n25\tFor example `\"d ?'cm'\"` matches a digit followed by an optional `cm` string.\n26\t\n27\tEach recognizer has three parts:\n28\t\n29\t```  \n30\t     *!u\n31\t     ^^^\n32\t     |||\n33\t     \\\\\\___ Recognizer\n34\t      \\\\___ Negate (optional)\n35\t       \\___ Repeat (optional, can be '*', '?' or '+')\n36\t```\n37\tNote that not all modifiers are meaningful for all recognizer.\n38\t\n39\tHere are some examples, next sections will detail the different types\n40\tof recognizers\n41\t```\n42\t    \"d\"            a single decimal digit\n43\t    \"dd\"           two digits\n44\t    \"+d\"           one or more digits\n45\t    \"?d\"           an optional digit\n46\t    \"dl\"           a digit follwed by a lower case ASCII letter\n47\t    \"d l\"          a digit follwed by a lower case ASCII letter\n48\t                   (spaces are meaningless)\n49\t    \"d S 'cm'\"     a digit followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n50\t    \"d S ?'cm'\"    a digit followed by optional spaces and an optional string \"`cm`\"\n51\t    \"d& S 'cm'\"    a digit followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n52\t                   but will match only the digit! (`&` is a *positive lookahead*)\n53\t    \"d!& S 'cm'\"   a digit *not* followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n54\t                   will match only the digit! (`!&` is a *negative lookahead*)\n55\t    \"`'` I `'`\"    An identifier between single quotes (backticks are used to \n56\t                   avoid clashes with the quote.)\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\t### Characters\n60\t\n61\tSince `skp` is aimed at parsing *technical* files (programs source code, data\n62\tfiles, etc..) we have restricted the concept of *letters* and *digit* to the\n63\tones encoded in ASCII.\n64\t\n65\t```\n66\t   .      any non \\0 character (match one codepoint if UTF-8 enabled)\n67\t   a      ASCII alphabetic char\n68\t   l      ASCII lower case\n69\t   u      ASCII upper case\n70\t   [...]  set (e.g.: [a-zα-ω] for a lowercase Latin or Greek letter); if ']' \n71\t          or '-' are part of the set, they must be the first or the last\n72\t          element in the set.\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\t### Numbers\n76\t\n77\tThese recognizers are useful when working with numbers:\n78\t\n79\t```\n80\t   d  ASCII decimal digit\n81\t   x  hex digit\n82\t   D  integer decimal number (possibly signed)\n83\t   F  floating point number (possibly with sign and exponent)\n84\t   X  hex number (possibly with leading 0x)\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t### Special characters\n88\t\n89\t```\n90\t   w  white space (includes some Unicode spaces)\n91\t   s  white space and vertical spaces (e.g. LF)\n92\t   c  control character (C0 and C1 Unicode blocks)\n93\t   n  a newline (`\\n`, `\\r` or `\\r\\n`)\n94\t   $  end of line (newline or end of text)\n95\t   !. (the \\0 character, i.e. the end of text)\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t### Identifiers\n99\t\n100\t```\n101\t   i  identifier character\n102\t   I  Identifier ([_A-Za-z][_0-9A-Za-z]*)\n103\t```\n104\t\n105\t### Strings\n106\t\n107\t```\n108\t   ' \" or ` a literal string\n109\t   Q  Quoted string with '\\' as escape\n110\t   B  Balanced sequence of parenthesis (can be '()', '[]', or '{}')\n111\t   () Balanced parenthesis (only '()')\n112\t   N  Anything up to the end of line\n113\t```\n114\t\n115\t### Modifiiers\n116\t\n117\t```\n118\t   C  case sensitive (ASCII) comparison\n119\t```\n120\t  By default, the match is case sensitive for ASCII strings you can \n121\tchange by adding `!C` to the pattern.\n122\t  To revert to case sensitive match, put `C` in the pattern.\n123\t\n124\t```\n125\t   U  utf-8 encoding (or ASCII/ISO-8859)\n126\t```\n127\t  By default, strings are considered to be a sequence of UTF-8 encoded\n128\tUnicode codepoints.\n129\t  You can switch to ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1) adding `!U` to the pattern.\n130\t\n131\t```\n132\t   >  skip to the start of pattern (skip to)\n133\t```\n134\t  See the section \"Skip to\" below.\n135\t\n136\t\n137\t```\n138\t   *  zero or more match\n139\t   ?  zero or one match\n140\t   +  one or more match\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\t```\n144\t   !  negate\n145\t\n146\t   &  set goal\n147\t   !& set negative goal\n148\t```\n149\t\n150\t### Pattern alternatives\n151\t\n152\t  You can try multiple (up to seven) patternes with a single `skp()` call.\n153\tThe value returned will tell which pattern has been matched.\n154\t\n155\t```\n156\t   \\1 ... \\7  alternatives\n157\t```\n158\t\n159\tExample:\n160\t\n161\t```\n162\t    n = skp(text,\"D\\3X\",end);\n163\t\n164\t    \"XYZ\" -> n will be 0\n165\t    \"123\" -> n will be 3\n166\t    \"A32\" -> n will be 1\n167\t```\n168\t\n169\t### String alternatives\n170\t  You can match multiple verbatim strings at once separating the with `\\xE`\n171\t\n172\t```\n173\t   \\xE string alternatives\n174\t```\n175\t\n176\tExample:\n177\t\n178\t```\n179\t    n = skp(text,\"D 'in\\xEcm'\",end);\n180\t```\n181\t  Will match an integer followed by either `in` or `cm`.\n182\t\n183\t## Level 1: Skipping\n184\t\n185\t### Basic skipping\n186\t\n187\tThe basic function for skipping text is:\n188\t\n189\t``` C\n190\t  int skp(char *start, char *pattern, char **to)\n191\t```\n192\t\n193\t  If the text at `start` matches the pattern `pattern`, `skp` will return `1` and the `to`\n194\tvariable will point right after the skipped text as shown in the picture below. \n195\t\n196\t```\n197\t        ╭─text that matches─╮\n198\t        │    the pattern    │\n199\t     ───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───\n200\t    ●●● │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  "}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8f6c0b2-be3a-4afc-b958-b08ccdcb843a","uuid":"d4e3b771-7eff-4599-93e0-eddb2d40b1f9","timestamp":"2026-06-03T08:30:07.320Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-nJX2RO/project/c_src/docs/index.md","content":"# skp\n\nSkp is a library of functions to help parsing text files.\n\nRather than answering the question \"which portion of the text match the pattern?\", skp\nanswer to the question \"which portion of the text can be skipped to match the pattern?\".\nThe difference is subtle but is there.\n\nSkp is *Unicode-aware* but, being the focus on parsing programming languages and data files,\nthe uppercase/lower case conversion is only for the ASCII portion of the Latin alphabet.\n\nSome of the Unicode spaces are recognized like U+00A0 (NO-BREAK SPACE), U+2001 (EM QUAD), etc.\n\nDefault encoding is UTF-8, alternatively the ISO-8859-1 encoding can be used.\n\nSkp can be used at three increasing levels of complexity:\n  - *Skipping*, where the focus is to identify portion of text that can be skipped according to a pattern;\n  - *Scanning*, where a text is repeatedly checked against a set of patterns;\n  - *Parsing*, where a Parsing Expression Grammar can be directly coded as a recursive descent parser.\n\n\n## Patterns\nTo match strings, `skp` uses *patterns*. \nA *pattern* is a sequence of *recognizers* possibly separated by spaces.\nFor example `\"d ?'cm'\"` matches a digit followed by an optional `cm` string.\n\nEach recognizer has three parts:\n\n```  \n     *!u\n     ^^^\n     |||\n     \\\\\\___ Recognizer\n      \\\\___ Negate (optional)\n       \\___ Repeat (optional, can be '*', '?' or '+')\n```\nNote that not all modifiers are meaningful for all recognizer.\n\nHere are some examples, next sections will detail the different types\nof recognizers\n```\n    \"d\"            a single decimal digit\n    \"dd\"           two digits\n    \"+d\"           one or more digits\n    \"?d\"           an optional digit\n    \"dl\"           a digit follwed by a lower case ASCII letter\n    \"d l\"          a digit follwed by a lower case ASCII letter\n                   (spaces are meaningless)\n    \"d S 'cm'\"     a digit followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n    \"d S ?'cm'\"    a digit followed by optional spaces and an optional string \"`cm`\"\n    \"d& S 'cm'\"    a digit followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n                   but will match only the digit! (`&` is a *positive lookahead*)\n    \"d!& S 'cm'\"   a digit *not* followed by optional spaces and the string \"`cm`\"\n                   will match only the digit! (`!&` is a *negative lookahead*)\n    \"`'` I `'`\"    An identifier between single quotes (backticks are used to \n                   avoid clashes with the quote.)\n```\n\n### Characters\n\nSince `skp` is aimed at parsing *technical* files (programs source code, data\nfiles, etc..) we have restricted the concept of *letters* and *digit* to the\nones encoded in ASCII.\n\n```\n   .      any non \\0 character (match one codepoint if UTF-8 enabled)\n   a      ASCII alphabetic char\n   l      ASCII lower case\n   u      ASCII upper case\n   [...]  set (e.g.: [a-zα-ω] for a lowercase Latin or Greek letter); if ']' \n          or '-' are part of the set, they must be the first or the last\n          element in the set.\n```\n\n### Numbers\n\nThese recognizers are useful when working with numbers:\n\n```\n   d  ASCII decimal digit\n   x  hex digit\n   D  integer decimal number (possibly signed)\n   F  floating point number (possibly with sign and exponent)\n   X  hex number (possibly with leading 0x)\n```\n\n### Special characters\n\n```\n   w  white space (includes some Unicode spaces)\n   s  white space and vertical spaces (e.g. LF)\n   c  control character (C0 and C1 Unicode blocks)\n   n  a newline (`\\n`, `\\r` or `\\r\\n`)\n   $  end of line (newline or end of text)\n   !. (the \\0 character, i.e. the end of text)\n```\n\n### Identifiers\n\n```\n   i  identifier character\n   I  Identifier ([_A-Za-z][_0-9A-Za-z]*)\n```\n\n### Strings\n\n```\n   ' \" or ` a literal string\n   Q  Quoted string with '\\' as escape\n   B  Balanced sequence of parenthesis (can be '()', '[]', or '{}')\n   () Balanced parenthesis (only '()')\n   N  Anything up to the end of line\n```\n\n### Modifiiers\n\n```\n   C  case sensitive (ASCII) comparison\n```\n  By default, the match is case sensitive for ASCII strings you can \nchange by adding `!C` to the pattern.\n  To revert to case sensitive match, put `C` in the pattern.\n\n```\n   U  utf-8 encoding (or ASCII/ISO-8859)\n```\n  By default, strings are considered to be a sequence of UTF-8 encoded\nUnicode codepoints.\n  You can switch to ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1) adding `!U` to the pattern.\n\n```\n   >  skip to the start of pattern (skip to)\n```\n  See the section \"Skip to\" below.\n\n\n```\n   *  zero or more match\n   ?  zero or one match\n   +  one or more match\n```\n\n```\n   !  negate\n\n   &  set goal\n   !& set negative goal\n```\n\n### Pattern alternatives\n\n  You can try multiple (up to seven) patternes with a single `skp()` call.\nThe value returned will tell which pattern has been matched.\n\n```\n   \\1 ... \\7  alternatives\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\n    n = skp(text,\"D\\3X\",end);\n\n    \"XYZ\" -> n will be 0\n    \"123\" -> n will be 3\n    \"A32\" -> n will be 1\n```\n\n### String alternatives\n  You can match multiple verbatim strings at once separating the with `\\xE`\n\n```\n   \\xE string alternatives\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\n    n = skp(text,\"D 'in\\xEcm'\",end);\n```\n  Will match an integer followed by either `in` or `cm`.\n\n## Level 1: Skipping\n\n### Basic skipping\n\nThe basic function for skipping text is:\n\n``` C\n  int skp(char *start, char *pattern, char **to)\n```\n\n  If the text at `start` matches the pattern `pattern`, `skp` will return `1` and the `to`\nvariable will point right after the skipped text as shown in the picture below. \n\n```\n        ╭─text that matches─╮\n        │    the pattern    │\n     ───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───\n    ●●● │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  ","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":732}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_c65atfgkywg4nyp3okem4kcdr4uo65uk24na723h5eh7yrgyqxrq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So `D 'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt'` is exactly the pattern from the test! It should match a number followed by either `cm`, `mm`, or `pt`. Looking at this, the `@` is just `is_alnum` matcher with default min=max=1. But there's something odd.\n\nThe example in the docs says `D 'in\\xEcm'`, but the test has `D @ 'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt'`. The `@` (alnum) matches one char. After `D` matches \"12\", we're at \"cm\". `@` matches 'c', advance to 'm'. Then `'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt'` checks 'm' against 'c' (no match), tries 'mm' alt... \n\nBut the **test expects this to match** with len=2. So the test expects something different — maybe @ should match no chars (or len computation should be just for D part).\n\nWait, the test passes if `len == 2` which means `to-from == 2`. So they expect to be at position 2 (right after \"12\"). That means D and @ and 'cm' should all match, but `to` should point just after the digits...\n\nLooking back at the C code: `to` is `s` (the s_off after all matching) by default. But wait — maybe the expectation is for the `&` (goal) marker. The test pattern is `D @ 'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt'` without `&`. With `&`, you'd say \"match D, then @ and 'cm/mm/pt' as lookahead — return position at end of D\".\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully: maybe the C engine has a bug in `is_string` handling alternatives, and the test expects the **fixed** behavior. Let me trace what it SHOULD do:\n\nFor \"12cm\" with `D @ 'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt'`:\n- D: matches \"12\", s_off = 2\n- @: matches 'c' (alnum), s_off = 3  \n- 'cm\\xEmm\\xEpt': at position 3 (s='m'), try 'cm' fail, try 'mm' fail, try 'pt' fail. So this should fail.\n\nHmm, but the test expects it to match. Actually wait — maybe I'm misreading and `@` is a lookahead/backtracking?  Let me re-examine — the docs say \"alphanumeric\" for `@`.\n\nLet me look more carefully at the docs to see if there's other interpretation:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4496,"cache_read_input_tokens":113313,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4496,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8f6c0b2-be3a-4afc-b958-b08ccdcb843a","uuid":"1b9c70e7-92cf-43e7-b6bf-d2a7d54a1814"}
