{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01DXqxGD11EkmeVCMvKELniF","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t    ●●● │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  \n201\t     ───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───\n202\t          ▲                   ▲\n203\t          ╰start              ╰to\n204\t\n205\t```\n206\t\n207\t  If there is no match, `skp` will return `0` and `to` will be set to `start`.\n208\t\n209\t  For example: \n210\t\n211\t``` C\n212\t  char *txt = \"Hello World\";\n213\t  char *to;\n214\t\n215\t  skp(txt,\"Hello \",&to);\n216\t  printf(\"%s\\n\",to); // will print \"World\"\n217\t\n218\t  skp(txt,\"Goodbye \",&to);\n219\t  printf(\"%s\\n\",to); // will print \"Hello World\" as there's nothing to skip.\n220\t  \n221\t```\n222\t\n223\t  Sometimes you just want to know if the text at `start` matches a certain pattern\n224\tor not. You can pass `NULL` as the value for `to` or use a simpler version of `skp()`:\n225\t\n226\t``` C\n227\t  int skp(char *start, char *pattern)\n228\t```\n229\t\n230\t  The following two lines are equivalent:\n231\t\n232\t``` C\n233\t  skp(mytext, mypattern, NULL);\n234\t  skp(mytext, mypattern);\n235\t```\n236\tExamples:\n237\t\n238\t``` C\n239\t   skp(cur,\"*s\", &cur);              // skip spaces (if any)\n240\t   skp(start,\"'|' *!'|' '|'\", &end); // skip text enclosed in '|'\n241\t   skp(text,\"!C'foo'\", &to);         // skips \"foo\", \"FoO\", \"fOo\", etc.\n242\t\n243\t   alt = skp(text,\"D\\1I\\2\");   // returns 1 if it's an integer number\n244\t                               //         2 if it's an identifier\n245\t                               //         0 None of the above\n246\t```\n247\t\n248\t\n249\t### Skip to\n250\tSometimes you want to skip ahead until you match a specified pattern:\n251\t\n252\t```\n253\t              ╭─text that matches─╮\n254\t              │    the pattern    │\n255\t   ───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───\n256\t  ●●● │   │   │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  \n257\t   ───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───\n258\t        ▲       ▲                   ▲  \n259\t        ╰start  ╰to                 ╰end\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\tFor example, you want to skip everything until you find an integer number.\n263\tTo achieve this result, you can place a `>` before your pattern like\n264\tin this example:\n265\t\n266\t``` C\n267\t   skp(text,\">D\",&num); // Set num to the the beginning of next number\n268\t```\n269\t\n270\tTo avoid calling `skp` again to reach the end of the text that matches\n271\tthe pattern, you can use another form of the `skp` function:\n272\t\n273\t``` C\n274\t  int skp(char *start, char *pattern, char** to, char **end)\n275\t```\n276\tIf there is no match, both `to` and `end` will be set equal to `start`.\n277\t\n278\tExample:\n279\t\n280\t``` C\n281\t   char *text = \"ab c 123 de\";\n282\t   char *to, *end;\n283\t\n284\t   skp(text, \">D\", &to, &end);\n285\t\n286\t   //   abxcy123pde\n287\t   //   ▲    ▲  ▲\n288\t   //   │    │  ╰── end\n289\t   //  text  ╰───── to\n290\t\n291\t```\n292\t\n293\t## Level 2: Scanning\n294\t  The `skp` function greatly simplifies extracting elements from a text.\n295\tSometimes, however, is more useful to continously match a block of text against\n296\ta set of patterns. If a match is successfull, you process the matching text\n297\tand move forward. This is a model followed, for example, by the `AWK` programming\n298\tlanguage. \n299\t\n300\t  There is a form of the `skp()` function that does this:\n301\t\n302\t``` C\n303\t    skp(char *text) { \n304\t      skpif( pat1 ) {\n305\t        /* code to run if match pat1 */\n306\t      }\n307\t      skpif( xxx ) {\n308\t        /* code to run if match pat1 */\n309\t      }\n310\t    }\n311\t```\n312\t\n313\tWithin the code guarded by `skp()` you can use the following *instructions*\n314\t``` C\n315\t    skpif(char *pattern) { ... }  // execute the code block if text match the pattern\n316\t    skpelse { }                   // execute the code if nothing matches\n317\t```\n318\t\n319\tand the following *read-only* variables:\n320\t```\n321\t    char   *skpfrom   pointer to the the start of the match\n322\t    char   *skpto     pointer to the the end of the match\n323\t    int32_t skplen    length of the matched text\n324\t```\n325\t\n326\tYou can think of it as loop that will go through the text until all\n327\tthe text has been scanned or no match is found.\n328\t\n329\tFor example, a simple CSV reader can be implemented with:\n330\t``` C\n331\t   skp(csv) {\n332\t     skpif(\"',' *s\") { num_col++; }  // Comma followed by spaces\n333\t     skpif(\"D\")      { store_number(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n334\t     skpif(\"Q\")      { store_string(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n335\t     skpif(\"*!','\")  { store_string(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n336\t     skpif(\"n\")      { num_row++; num_col = 0; } // Newline\n337\t     skpif(\"+s\")     { } // Skip spaces\n338\t     skpelse         { prterror(\"Unexpected text at %d,%d\\n\",num_row, num_col); \n339\t                       break;\n340\t                     }\n341\t     last = skpto; // Store position\n342\t   }\n343\t   if (*last != '\\0') { // abnormal exit }\n344\t```\n345\t\n346\t## Level 3: Parsing\n347\t\n348\tFor the most complicated case, you may want to define a real *parser* for the \n349\ttype of text you need to process. \n350\tThe functions (and macros) available in `skp` allow you to define a recursive\n351\tdescent parser using a Parser Expression Grammar (PEG) directly represented\n352\tas C code.\n353\t\n354\t  The main idea behind the implementation of this feature is in the following article:\n355\t\n356\t```\n357\t   R. R. Redziejowski. Parsing Expression Grammar as a Primitive\n358\t   Recursive-Descent Parser with Backtracking.\n359\t   Fundamenta Informaticae 79, 3-4 (2007), pp. 513 - 524. \n360\t   http://www.romanredz.se/papers/FI2007.pdf\n361\t```\n362\t\n363\t  As with many other PEG based parser, left recursive grammars are not supported\n364\tand would lead to an infinite loop. If such loop is detected, the parser will stop\n365\treturning an error.\n366\t\n367\t  Parser defined with `skp` are both compact and fast for practical, not overly\n368\tcomplicated, grammars. They use partial memoization to provides (but \n369\tnot guarantee) a quasi-linear parsing time with reasonable memory consumption.\n370\t\n371\t  In the following, we'll assume familiarity with PEG and recursive descent parsers.\n372\t\n373\t### Grammars, terminals and non-terminals.\n374\t\n375\t  A `skp` parser tries to mimic closely the structure of the grammar it\n376\tis intended to parse. Once the grammar terminals and non-terminals have been defined,\n377\ta text can be parsed using the function on the starting rule:\n378\t```\n379\t   ast_t skpparse(char *text, skprule_t rule [, int debug])\n380\t```\n381\twhich returns the corresponding *Abstract Syntax Tree* (an object of type `ast_t`).\n382\t\n383\t  Properly building and traversing the AST is the key for using `skp` parsers at their\n384\tfullest potential, I reccomend to carefully read the sections about AST.\n385\t\n386\t\n387\t  A `skp` *rule* represent a non-terminal in the grammar and is defined as if it\n388\twas C function (well, it *is* a C function ...):\n389\t```\n390\t  skpdef(rule) {\n391\t     /* rule body */\n392\t  }\n393\t```\n394\t\n395\t  Within the definition of a rule, you can reference other rules (non-terminals) \n396\tuaing the function:\n397\t```\n398\t  void skprule(skprule_t rule)\n399\t``` "}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8f6c0b2-be3a-4afc-b958-b08ccdcb843a","uuid":"72ba7159-6e69-4c0f-9774-6830d2fd2a6f","timestamp":"2026-06-03T08:32:07.409Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-nJX2RO/project/c_src/docs/index.md","content":"    ●●● │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  \n     ───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───\n          ▲                   ▲\n          ╰start              ╰to\n\n```\n\n  If there is no match, `skp` will return `0` and `to` will be set to `start`.\n\n  For example: \n\n``` C\n  char *txt = \"Hello World\";\n  char *to;\n\n  skp(txt,\"Hello \",&to);\n  printf(\"%s\\n\",to); // will print \"World\"\n\n  skp(txt,\"Goodbye \",&to);\n  printf(\"%s\\n\",to); // will print \"Hello World\" as there's nothing to skip.\n  \n```\n\n  Sometimes you just want to know if the text at `start` matches a certain pattern\nor not. You can pass `NULL` as the value for `to` or use a simpler version of `skp()`:\n\n``` C\n  int skp(char *start, char *pattern)\n```\n\n  The following two lines are equivalent:\n\n``` C\n  skp(mytext, mypattern, NULL);\n  skp(mytext, mypattern);\n```\nExamples:\n\n``` C\n   skp(cur,\"*s\", &cur);              // skip spaces (if any)\n   skp(start,\"'|' *!'|' '|'\", &end); // skip text enclosed in '|'\n   skp(text,\"!C'foo'\", &to);         // skips \"foo\", \"FoO\", \"fOo\", etc.\n\n   alt = skp(text,\"D\\1I\\2\");   // returns 1 if it's an integer number\n                               //         2 if it's an identifier\n                               //         0 None of the above\n```\n\n\n### Skip to\nSometimes you want to skip ahead until you match a specified pattern:\n\n```\n              ╭─text that matches─╮\n              │    the pattern    │\n   ───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───┬───\n  ●●● │   │   │▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│▒▒▒│   │   │ ●●●  \n   ───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───┴───\n        ▲       ▲                   ▲  \n        ╰start  ╰to                 ╰end\n```\n\nFor example, you want to skip everything until you find an integer number.\nTo achieve this result, you can place a `>` before your pattern like\nin this example:\n\n``` C\n   skp(text,\">D\",&num); // Set num to the the beginning of next number\n```\n\nTo avoid calling `skp` again to reach the end of the text that matches\nthe pattern, you can use another form of the `skp` function:\n\n``` C\n  int skp(char *start, char *pattern, char** to, char **end)\n```\nIf there is no match, both `to` and `end` will be set equal to `start`.\n\nExample:\n\n``` C\n   char *text = \"ab c 123 de\";\n   char *to, *end;\n\n   skp(text, \">D\", &to, &end);\n\n   //   abxcy123pde\n   //   ▲    ▲  ▲\n   //   │    │  ╰── end\n   //  text  ╰───── to\n\n```\n\n## Level 2: Scanning\n  The `skp` function greatly simplifies extracting elements from a text.\nSometimes, however, is more useful to continously match a block of text against\na set of patterns. If a match is successfull, you process the matching text\nand move forward. This is a model followed, for example, by the `AWK` programming\nlanguage. \n\n  There is a form of the `skp()` function that does this:\n\n``` C\n    skp(char *text) { \n      skpif( pat1 ) {\n        /* code to run if match pat1 */\n      }\n      skpif( xxx ) {\n        /* code to run if match pat1 */\n      }\n    }\n```\n\nWithin the code guarded by `skp()` you can use the following *instructions*\n``` C\n    skpif(char *pattern) { ... }  // execute the code block if text match the pattern\n    skpelse { }                   // execute the code if nothing matches\n```\n\nand the following *read-only* variables:\n```\n    char   *skpfrom   pointer to the the start of the match\n    char   *skpto     pointer to the the end of the match\n    int32_t skplen    length of the matched text\n```\n\nYou can think of it as loop that will go through the text until all\nthe text has been scanned or no match is found.\n\nFor example, a simple CSV reader can be implemented with:\n``` C\n   skp(csv) {\n     skpif(\"',' *s\") { num_col++; }  // Comma followed by spaces\n     skpif(\"D\")      { store_number(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n     skpif(\"Q\")      { store_string(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n     skpif(\"*!','\")  { store_string(skpfrom, skpto, num_row, num_col);}\n     skpif(\"n\")      { num_row++; num_col = 0; } // Newline\n     skpif(\"+s\")     { } // Skip spaces\n     skpelse         { prterror(\"Unexpected text at %d,%d\\n\",num_row, num_col); \n                       break;\n                     }\n     last = skpto; // Store position\n   }\n   if (*last != '\\0') { // abnormal exit }\n```\n\n## Level 3: Parsing\n\nFor the most complicated case, you may want to define a real *parser* for the \ntype of text you need to process. \nThe functions (and macros) available in `skp` allow you to define a recursive\ndescent parser using a Parser Expression Grammar (PEG) directly represented\nas C code.\n\n  The main idea behind the implementation of this feature is in the following article:\n\n```\n   R. R. Redziejowski. Parsing Expression Grammar as a Primitive\n   Recursive-Descent Parser with Backtracking.\n   Fundamenta Informaticae 79, 3-4 (2007), pp. 513 - 524. \n   http://www.romanredz.se/papers/FI2007.pdf\n```\n\n  As with many other PEG based parser, left recursive grammars are not supported\nand would lead to an infinite loop. If such loop is detected, the parser will stop\nreturning an error.\n\n  Parser defined with `skp` are both compact and fast for practical, not overly\ncomplicated, grammars. They use partial memoization to provides (but \nnot guarantee) a quasi-linear parsing time with reasonable memory consumption.\n\n  In the following, we'll assume familiarity with PEG and recursive descent parsers.\n\n### Grammars, terminals and non-terminals.\n\n  A `skp` parser tries to mimic closely the structure of the grammar it\nis intended to parse. Once the grammar terminals and non-terminals have been defined,\na text can be parsed using the function on the starting rule:\n```\n   ast_t skpparse(char *text, skprule_t rule [, int debug])\n```\nwhich returns the corresponding *Abstract Syntax Tree* (an object of type `ast_t`).\n\n  Properly building and traversing the AST is the key for using `skp` parsers at their\nfullest potential, I reccomend to carefully read the sections about AST.\n\n\n  A `skp` *rule* represent a non-terminal in the grammar and is defined as if it\nwas C function (well, it *is* a C function ...):\n```\n  skpdef(rule) {\n     /* rule body */\n  }\n```\n\n  Within the definition of a rule, you can reference other rules (non-terminals) \nuaing the function:\n```\n  void skprule(skprule_t rule)\n``` ","numLines":200,"startLine":200,"totalLines":732}}}
